Why this exists
===============

Getting a machine should take a pull request, not a favour.

Most community servers run on one person's memory: they set it up, they
know the password, and when they're busy nobody gets in. mothership turns
that into a process anyone can read. You write a file, someone reviews it,
and the server builds itself. The goal is that a human touches the box at
most once per member: the review.


Git is the guest list
---------------------

Your access is user-vms/you.nix. Merge it and Nix builds your VM, keys,
quota and public URL from it. Delete it and they're gone. `git log` answers
who has access, who approved it, when and why. There's no separate user
database to drift out of date.

Web apps like Mattermost still have their own logins, since git can't sign
you into a browser. That's fine for now.


Why not the cloud?
------------------

A used 2016 server does the work of a few thousand dollars a month of
cloud. Cost isn't the main reason, though. The cloud teaches you one
company's buttons and its billing page. A server in the room teaches you
watts, disks, packets and boot order: things that stay true everywhere.

And when you can't buy more compute, you make your code better.

We keep it simple on purpose: one server, one config, one firewall, one
switch. There's no Kubernetes and no high availability. If it's down, it's
down, and finding out why is the lesson.


Breakable because rebuildable
-----------------------------

Nothing on the server is precious. The disk array is running degraded and
we've left it that way. A member can wreck their VM, and an operator can
wreck the host.

That's fine, because git can put it all back. So we don't keep score with
uptime. The score is how many minutes it takes to go from wiped to working,
using only git. We measure that, publish it, and try to make it smaller.
See docs/drills.md.


Why the docs came back
----------------------

We once deleted the docs so people would read the Nix instead of pasting a
wiki into a chatbot. That was a mistake. It mostly shut out the people who
were trying to learn.

So docs/ is back, with one rule: the docs explain, the Nix decides. Every
page points at the file that's the real source of truth. If a doc and the
code disagree, the code wins, and the doc is a bug you can fix in your PR.


Where to start
--------------

1. Get a machine (README.md). It takes five minutes.
2. Read docs/ in order.
3. Open hosts/mothership/ and follow the imports.
4. Break your own VM. Then help us break the server on purpose, and time
   how long it takes to come back.

Ask questions anytime. Pointing at the line that confused you is the
fastest way to get a good answer.
